# System clock builtins

`System.nanoTime()` and `System.currentTimeMillis()` are core builtins on the v1
interpreter. On the v2 native lane the front emits a method call on the bare
`System` receiver, so the runtime must resolve `System.nanoTime` as a registered
native — otherwise it performs an effect nobody handles and the program dies with
`unhandled runtime effect: System.nanoTime`. That silently killed the entire
`ssc bench --backend v2` lane, whose generated wrapper times itself this way.

Values are non-deterministic, so assert only the invariants: elapsed monotonic
time is non-negative, and wall-clock epoch millis are past 2020-01-01.

```scalascript
val t0 = System.nanoTime()
val t1 = System.nanoTime()
println(t1 - t0 >= 0)

val millis = System.currentTimeMillis()
println(millis > 1577836800000L)
```
